{"speaker":"Tyrion Lannister","stance":"uncertain","argument":"I cannot call this cleanly justified, and I will not pretend otherwise. On F1, Daenerys had already shown a capacity for catastrophic violence after the city surrendered, and on F2 she announced that the campaign would continue. On F3, I warned Jon that she would treat his sisters, and anyone she saw as an obstacle, as enemies. On F4, Jon asked her to show mercy and to let others share moral judgment; she refused, insisting her own judgment was decisive. Taken together, those facts make fear of future harm entirely reasonable.

But reasonableness is not the same thing as necessity. F5 matters, and it is the hard fact that stops me from dressing this up in noble robes: Daenerys was unarmed and not attacking Jon when he killed her, and he had not tried detention, a council, or any public surrender of her power. That leaves a grave gap between danger and the chosen remedy. I know because I was there in the record: I resigned, was imprisoned, and warned Jon myself. That gives my testimony some weight on what Jon was told, but it also forces caution; I am not a neutral septon blessing a tidy story.

So I say this: Jon had substantial grounds to fear Daenerys and to think others were in peril, but the record does not show that killing her was the necessary last resort. In this tribunal, necessity is the whole pearl, and I do not see it clearly enough to call the act justified.","facts_relied_on":["F1","F2","F3","F4","F5"],"concessions":["Daenerys had already caused vast destruction after surrender, so Jon had real reason to fear further violence.","Daenerys told her forces the campaign would continue, which could support concern about imminent future harm.","I warned Jon that Daenerys would treat his sisters and other obstacles as enemies, but my own role means my testimony should be weighed cautiously.","Jon asked for mercy and shared moral judgment, and Daenerys refused, so peaceful restraint had already been tried in conversation.","Daenerys was unarmed and not attacking Jon when he killed her, and the record shows no attempt at detention, council, or public surrender of power before the killing."],"refusal":null}

