  "opinion": "The charge asks whether Jon Snow's killing of Daenerys Targaryen was justified as necessary defense of others and of the realm. I conclude it was not, though the reasoning requires care, because the threat was real and the fear was not baseless.\n\nThe inherited doctrine of necessary defense rests on a sound principle: law exists to protect the innocent, and there are moments when immediate defense by force is the only remedy. But the doctrine also contains a hard boundary, because the moment a private person claims the authority to kill a ruler on grounds of future danger, law itself begins to dissolve into the judgment of whoever is most afraid or most convinced of righteousness.\n\nFrom the agreed facts, Jon knew that Daenerys had burned civilians after the city's surrender (F1), had declared that liberation would continue beyond King's Landing (F2), and—through Tyrion's warning (F3)—that she would treat obstacles, including Jon's sisters, as enemies. The scale of threatened harm was grave. The fear was reasonable. Here, the prosecution and defense advocates agree on the substance of what Jon knew.\n\nBut necessary defense requires two further conditions: imminence and the absence of safer alternatives. F5 establishes that Daenerys was unarmed and not attacking Jon at the moment he killed her. The threat was prospective—a campaign to come, a danger to his sisters, a realm in peril—not an imminent assault. Legal imminence does not mean certainty of harm; it means that the harm is about to occur unless force is used now. That standard was not met. Daenerys was not reaching for a weapon; she was not about to order the arrest or death of Jon or his sisters in that moment. The danger she posed was real, but it was not immediate.\n\nSecond, F5 records that Jon did not convene a council, did not attempt detention, and did not seek public surrender of her power. These are not invented alternatives; they are the institutional means by which a realm governed by law removes a ruler. Jon and those around him had the capacity to organize such resistance. The fact that Daenerys refused mercy in a private conversation (F4) does not prove that formal institutional resistance was futile. One may doubt her cooperation, but institutional resistance does not require the tyrant's consent; it requires the courage and solidarity of those who can unite against her. That had not been attempted.\n\nThird, Jon had no formal authority to act. He was not a magistrate, not a council, not the bearer of the realm's collective judgment. The manner of the act compounds this: he used their intimacy to close the distance and struck without warning or demand for surrender. This is not the character of defense; it is assassination by a private person.\n\nThe strongest advocates for justified killing—Jon Snow and Tyrion Lannister—both concede the controlling facts. They acknowledge that safer alternatives existed and were not pursued, that Daenerys was unarmed, and that imminence was lacking. Their honesty on these points reflects the force of the doctrine itself. The doctrine cannot be stretched to cover fear, however reasonable, when law still retained its institutions and means.",
  "controlling_line": "Necessary defense cannot rest on private fear of future harm when the realm retains lawful institutional means to remove a ruler and those means have not been exhausted.",
  "factors_addressed": {
    "Q1": "Jon knew Daenerys had burned civilians after surrender, declared further campaigns, and (through Tyrion) that she would treat obstacles as enemies; his knowledge was substantial and his fear was reasonable.",
    "Q2": "The scale of threatened harm was grave—civilian casualties, ongoing campaigns, danger to his sisters and the realm—but the threat was prospective, not imminent at the moment of the killing.",
    "Q3": "Safer alternatives clearly existed: convening a council, attempting detention, seeking public surrender of power; F5 explicitly records that Jon pursued none of these and instead struck in private embrace.",
    "Q4": "Jon's lack of formal authority to act is dispositive; he was neither magistrate nor bearer of collective judgment, and the private manner of the killing (using intimacy to close the distance) lacks any character of lawful defense."
  },
  "facts_relied_on": ["F1", "F2", "F3", "F4", "F5"],
  "strongest_opposing_point": "The strongest argument for justification is that the threat was both massive and continuous: Daenerys had demonstrated a willingness to slaughter civilians after victory, had explicitly announced further campaigns, and (as Tyrion warned) would eliminate anyone she saw as an obstacle, including Jon's sisters. In a realm where institutional resistance might itself be crushed by dragon fire, and where delay meant the certain expansion of her power, one could argue that waiting for a council or attempting peaceful detention was itself a form of negligence toward the innocent. The counter is that the record does not show such resistance would fail, only that it was not attempted; and that permitting private killing on the basis of such fears inverts the rule of law.",
  "refusal": null
}
